# Workshop Architecture Invariants

## One-way dependency graph

- Dependencies flow one way: server -> features -> services -> vocabulary. Never add a dependency from a lower tier to a higher one. If Cargo rejects a cycle, the design is wrong, not the graph.
- Tiers: vocabulary (`workshop-protocol`, `workshop-registry`, `workshop-support`) <- services (`workshop-gateway`, `workshop-menu`, `workshop-status`) <- features (`workshop-user-state`, `workshop-workspace`) <- server (`workshop-server`). The sessions subsystem has no crate of its own; it lives inside `workshop-server`.
- Crates in the same tier never depend on each other, with one exception: `workshop-registry` depends on `workshop-protocol`, because the status push-channel slot holds `workshop-protocol`'s `StatusBarUpdate`. Otherwise same-tier crates meet through `workshop-protocol` wire types and `workshop-registry` proxy slots.
- The desktop app (package `workshop`) sits outside the tiers and depends on `workshop-server-api`, never on `workshop-server`; the facade is its entire view of the server. `cargo test -p build-xtask` enforces this.
- Every `workshop-*` crate's `lib.rs` opens with a `//!` doc carrying a `## Invariants` marker that lists what the crate may depend on and what it may not. Read it before adding an import.
- `lib.rs` is a facade only: crate docs, crate-level attributes, `mod` declarations, and `pub use` re-exports. No logic.

## Registry pattern

- Subsystems self-register into `workshop-registry` proxy slots: routes, state handles, background tasks, push channels, and shutdown handles. The composition root (`crates/workshop/server/src/app/compose.rs`) calls each subsystem's `register` by name and requires its concrete handle, so a missing registration fails boot naming the type. Subsystems reach each other only through the registry.
- Unregistered slots are graceful no-ops. Never add a hard dependency on an optional subsystem.
- Registration handles are `#[must_use]`; registry traits are sealed so only workshop crates implement them.

## File ceiling

- No file exceeds 500 lines. If an edit would push a file past 500, split first, then edit. `cargo test -p build-xtask` enforces the ceiling, the tier graph, and lint inheritance over the Rust files in the workshop crates carrying the marker; the desktop app (the `workshop` crate) is exempt until the headless agent mode plan.
